{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788265279,"nonce":"VmEhoK7XzmqVuixWGuZDm/r9qEg=","title":"An unknown argument name is silently ignored and falls back to a default, so a wrong arg reports success","message":"Found while running session 13's live acceptance on bench `20260901T121508`. Not a regression — long-standing, and it produced three wrong results in a row before I noticed.\n\n## What happened\n\n`dev:spawn-thing`'s cell argument is `pos`. I passed `at`, which is the OUTPUT key name and the obvious guess:\n\n    dev:spawn-thing {\"def\":\"SimpleResearchBench\",\"pos\":…}   \u003c- correct\n    dev:spawn-thing {\"def\":\"SimpleResearchBench\",\"at\":…}    \u003c- silently ignored\n\n`Dev.PosArg(map, a, \"pos\")` reads `args.Raw(\"pos\")`, gets null, and returns `Anchor(map)` — the first free colonist's cell, else map centre. So three consecutive spawns targeting `[999,999]`, `[107,119]` and `[90,90]` all landed at `[125,129]` and all reported `ok:true, placed:1`. Nothing in any envelope said an argument had been dropped.\n\nI diagnosed it as \"`direct` mode ignores its cell argument\" — a plausible and entirely wrong conclusion — and only found the real cause by reading `DevVerbs.cs`. An agent without source access would have filed that wrong bug.\n\n## Why this is worth fixing rather than documenting\n\nThe project has already recorded this failure class twice. `accept/2.5-png-render.md` trap 6: *\"a wrong argument name here is silent\"*. The `things`-has-no-`filter` trap is the same shape. Both were found by dry-running against a stub, not on a bench — because on a bench they look like success.\n\nA default that fires on a typo is worse than no default: it turns a caller error into a plausible-looking result, and the result is journalled as though it were intended.\n\n## Scope\n\n**Reject unknown argument keys.** A verb should know its own arg names and refuse a call carrying one it does not recognise, with `bad-args` naming the unknown key and, where the edit distance is small, the key it probably meant (`at` -\u003e `pos` is the worked example).\n\nInvestigate where this belongs — `VerbArgs` is the obvious home since every verb goes through it, but that means every verb needs a declared arg set, and some may take open-ended payloads. Read `VerbRegistry.cs` and `VerbArgs` before choosing. If a whitelist per verb is too invasive, a narrower fix is acceptable: any verb that has a positional/cell default must report which source the value came from (`pos_source: \"arg\" | \"anchor-default\"`), so the fallback is visible in the envelope and in the journal.\n\n**Consider whether `Anchor(map)` should be a default at all** for a cell argument. \"Put it where the first colonist is standing\" is a reasonable default for a starter kit and an unreasonable one for a targeted spawn; the two callers want different things and currently share a fallback.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- A verb call carrying an unrecognised argument key is refused with `bad-args` naming the key.\n- The refusal suggests the intended key where the edit distance is small; prove it with `at` -\u003e `pos` on `dev:spawn-thing`.\n- No existing acceptance suite breaks — run them; several pass args this change might newly reject, and that is the real cost to measure before committing to the whitelist form.\n- If the narrower fix is chosen instead: `dev:spawn-thing` reports which source its cell came from, and the journal row carries it.","files":null}]}